<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Engine.Renderer</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            Where the engine's scene meets the renderer: SceneRenderHost, which turns a compositor
            document, a device and a render system into a recorded frame, and the renderer that drains a
            DebugDraw once a frame into two line draws.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>debug-draw;diagnostics;overlay;rendering;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            The two halves this assembly exists to join, and the reason it is a separate assembly
            rather than a folder in either of them — the same arrangement as Vixen.Ui.Renderer and
            for the same reason. `Vixen.Engine` accumulates debug geometry and knows nothing about a
            device, which is what lets physics, navigation and audio produce it without linking a
            graphics API; `Vixen.Rendering` draws lines without knowing what a debug overlay is.
            Putting the join in `Vixen.Engine` would drag a graphics API into the layer a game is
            written against; putting it in `Vixen.Rendering` would drag the ECS into every renderer.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Engine\Vixen.Engine.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Rendering\Vixen.Rendering.csproj" />

        <!--
            Content, for AssetMeshSource. This is the third join this assembly exists to make and the
            same shape as the other two: `Vixen.Rendering` must not know what a bundle is — its business
            is a vertex buffer — and `Vixen.Assets` must not know what a mesh buffer is. Putting the
            join in either drags one whole layer into the other.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Assets\Vixen.Assets.csproj" />

        <!--
            And the container a texture artefact is written in, for AssetTextureSource. Decoding a KTX2
            is the one piece of a material's textures that is neither content nor rendering: the bytes
            come from a bundle and the pixels go to a device, and this is what sits between them.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Imaging\Vixen.Core.Imaging.csproj" />

        <!--
            The terrain's device side, for AssetTerrainTextures. This assembly is where the asset
            system and the render system meet, which is exactly what ITerrainTextures needs one of
            each of — see its own remarks for why the renderer does not hold an asset manager.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Rendering.Terrain\Vixen.Rendering.Terrain.csproj" />

        <!--
            And water's, for AssetWaterSource — the same join once more. A water body names its
            spline and a zone names its sea state, both by name, and `WaterZoneSystem` deliberately
            takes them through seams rather than holding an asset manager: a test's spline is a
            literal and an editor's is a document being dragged. This is where the game's answer
            lives.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Rendering.Water\Vixen.Rendering.Water.csproj" />

        <!--
            The interface's device half, for WorldRenderer.Ui. ⚠ This is the fourth join and the one
            that had no seat: `Vixen.Ui.Renderer` exists so that neither `Vixen.Ui` nor
            `Vixen.Rendering` has to know about the other, and it was written for a host that would
            put a `UiRenderFeature` in a scene's render system — a host that did not exist, so the
            feature had no caller at all and whether it worked had never been observed.

            The direction is the allowed one and it is worth saying which. What `CheckArchitecture`
            refuses is `Vixen.Ui*` → `Vixen.Engine`: a UI framework that needs a world and a game
            loop is not a UI framework. A renderer that can draw an interface is not that boundary
            crossed, it is the boundary used — the same shape as the debug-draw join this assembly
            was created for.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Ui.Renderer\Vixen.Ui.Renderer.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Engine.Renderer.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
